This paper introduces ATOMIC, a comprehensive dataset designed to support the development of neural models capable of performing if-then reasoning. The dataset comprises 1.2 million instances of if-then reasoning tasks, each accompanied by a detailed explanation of the underlying reasoning process. These instances are derived from 10,000 abstracts sourced from the ACL Anthology, which were annotated by 100 human experts. To facilitate the creation of this extensive dataset, we developed a novel annotation tool that enables experts to annotate reasoning processes for abstracts in a straightforward manner. Furthermore, we present a new neural model that leverages the ATOMIC dataset to learn if-then reasoning. Experimental results indicate that this model significantly outperforms existing approaches on the ATOMIC dataset and achieves state-of-the-art performance on the SNLI dataset. Additionally, we demonstrate that the model can be trained on a smaller dataset and still surpasses current methods on the ATOMIC dataset. Finally, we provide a detailed analysis of the ATOMIC dataset and the model's reasoning capabilities. The dataset is available at http://atomic.csail.mit.edu/.
